From 2bc119d6394ea3dcb12194b61be9754446306906 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Ming Lei <ming.lei@clyso.com>
Date: Thu, 29 Oct 2026 04:11:52 -0500
Subject: [PATCH 2/3] os/bluestore: admit an onode to the cache only on its
 second use

The onode cache is an LRU: every onode loaded or created goes to its
front and stays until the cache is full. One pass over many objects (a
deep scrub, backfill, recovery, or writes of objects that are not read
back) fills the whole cache with onodes that will not be used again, and
the cache tuner lets it, since it sees only the mempool bytes, about half
of the real heap cost of a small object's onode. Measured on a 2-OSD
cluster with a 4 GiB osd_memory_target: RGW 4 KiB PUTs drove an OSD to
4.7 GiB, 3.0 GiB of it onodes that the phase never touched again.

Keep new onodes on a bounded probation FIFO (a
bluestore_onode_cache_probation_ratio share of the shard, default 0.1)
and move one to the main LRU only when it is used again in a later cache
age bin, so the touches of one op sequence do not count. Probation is
trimmed first; the main LRU behaves as before. The age bins now rotate
without the autotuner too. A new perf counter, onodes_probation, shows
the split.
